WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.110
&gt;&gt; Hello.

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.100
This is Lydia Fleischman from
NCBI.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:07.760
And on behalf of the team, I
wanted to greet everyone here in

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.549
the Spheres meeting, and thank
you, Duncan, for having us.

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:14.610
We'll be having a couple of
people from our team speak today

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.890
about sequence submissions,
specifically of SARS-CoV-2

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:22.650
sequences, both unassembled and
assembled, as well as a great

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.420
annotation tool, VADR, which
Eric Nawrocki will be speaking

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:27.650
about.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:31.770
And then we also just kind of
wanted to emphasize the dialogue

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.161
that we want to keep open with
this community and with the

00:00:34.161 --> 00:00:35.320
greater community.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.190
Eric, if you could go to the
next slide.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.400
We wanted to engage with you and
really continue a dialogue on

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.180
NCBI submission.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.690
We want your feedback on our
existing processes and data and

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:47.989
tools.

00:00:47.989 --> 00:00:52.420
And so, we really welcome this
opportunity to do a refresher of

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:57.010
what we have for SARS-CoV-2
submission to SRA and GenBank,

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.291
introduce that helpful tool for
annotation that I mentioned, and

00:01:00.291 --> 00:01:05.489
share a little bit about ongoing
opportunities for feedback and

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:09.070
interaction with the NCBI team.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.670
And with that, I will pass it
over to Eric.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:17.560
&gt;&gt; Okay, thanks, Lydia.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.240
So, my name's Eric Nawrocki.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.020
I work at NCBI.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.729
I'm going to be talking about
VADR, which is the software tool

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:30.270
that I'm the lead developer of
that is used for SARS-CoV-2

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.720
analysis.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.790
So, we use it to validate and
annotate incoming sequences.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.770
So, it's a general tool that you
can really use for any type of

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.770
viral sequence.

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:49.830
And it does the annotation and
validation based on a reference.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:53.479
I'm going to go into detail as
to how it works, but at a high

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:57.119
level, it does that by aligning
sequences globally with respect

00:01:57.119 --> 00:01:58.119
to the RefSeq.

00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:02.170
It can also use a profile model
for SARS-CoV-2.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.400
We just use a single RefSeq, but
it's designed to use profiles

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.400
also.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.470
So those are models built from
an alignment of sequences --

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:13.390
representative sequences instead
of a single sequence.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:17.230
We originally tested it for
dengue virus and norovirus, but

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:22.510
it's been in use for SARS-CoV-2
since March on a kind of manual

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.510
base.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:26.580
It was being used manually
early and then switched to a

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.920
more automated pipeline in June.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:36.640
So, this work was published in
BMC Bioinformatics earlier this

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.530
year.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:46.290
Okay, so, to kind of introduce
the motivation for why we

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:51.690
developed VADR, I'm going to
talk about kind of how sequence

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.709
submissions go just very
briefly.

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:58.790
So, at NCBI, at GenBank, we have
indexers.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.660
Linda Yankie is one.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.660
Vince Calhoun is another who
deals with SARS-CoV-2 sequences.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.569
And they take the incoming
sequences and metadata from the

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:12.190
submitter and are in charge of
determining if they should be

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:17.330
accepted into GenBank or if
they should be returned to the

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.630
submitter for further validation
or because there's some issue

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:21.630
with them.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:29.690
And so, we developed VADR as an
annotation tool for viruses,

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:32.030
specifically to help the
indexer.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:36.270
So we know that there's other
viral annotation software out

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.020
there, some excellent software
that we could have used.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:43.120
So, VIGR is an example, VIPR,
VAPiD.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.370
These are really great tools,
but we decided to develop our

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:52.209
own software because we wanted
very precise control over which

00:03:52.209 --> 00:03:57.269
sequences would automatically be
let into GenBank versus -- and

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:01.900
we call those passing sequences
versus sequences that have to be

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:05.379
looked at by a human indexer.

00:04:05.379 --> 00:04:08.659
So it's really important that we
don't want to let any sequences

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:11.220
that are bad automatically into
GenBank.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.600
So VADR identifies many
different types of issues with

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.189
sequences and kind of unusual
features of sequences, some of

00:04:19.189 --> 00:04:22.639
which may be innocuous, and the
sequence is perfectly

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:29.089
biologically valid, but those
situations warrant the sequence

00:04:29.089 --> 00:04:32.819
to be looked at by an expert
indexer.

00:04:32.819 --> 00:04:35.800
So, that's what VADR does.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.289
It passes some sequences
automatically, and others are

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:41.180
going to fail and be looked at
by the indexer.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.960
And the indexer then looks at
the information returned from

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.559
VADR and determines if their
sequences should be accepted

00:04:48.559 --> 00:04:51.259
because they look fine or if
they should be returned to the

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:52.580
submitter.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.210
And I'm not showing another
arrow here, but there could be

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:58.509
an arrow here where VADR
determines that some sequences

00:04:58.509 --> 00:05:05.210
are kind of so obviously messed
up in some way that they should

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:08.389
automatically be returned to the
submitter without an indexer

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:09.569
having to look at them.

00:05:09.569 --> 00:05:12.599
So, some kind of obvious
problems.

00:05:12.599 --> 00:05:17.629
So, now I'm going to go into
kind of how VADR works.

00:05:17.629 --> 00:05:23.580
And the first step of VADR is to
build a homology model of the

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:28.169
virus you're trying to annotate
and validate for.

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:30.729
So, we do this using RefSeq
currently.

00:05:30.729 --> 00:05:34.909
So, this is an example using
dengue, but everything I'm going

00:05:34.909 --> 00:05:37.310
to talk about applies also to
SARS-CoV-2.

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:42.330
So, these are all of the
features in the GenBank record

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.860
for one of the RefSeqs for
dengue, the type 1.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:51.370
And I'm showing you a CDS
feature, the gene which is

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:54.680
essentially the same as the CDS
as far as coordinates, the

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.189
mat-peptides that the CDS is
broken down into, the mature

00:05:59.189 --> 00:06:00.679
peptides, rather.

00:06:00.679 --> 00:06:04.729
And there's also secondary
structural elements in the

00:06:04.729 --> 00:06:11.341
dengue genome that are features
in the RefSeq, and those are

00:06:11.341 --> 00:06:13.279
actually modeled by VADR.

00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:18.580
So, when we build a model for
VADR, we build a profile model

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:22.349
called a covariance model that
includes both sequence

00:06:22.349 --> 00:06:24.810
information and secondary
structure information.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:28.860
And so, when it does analysis,
it can take that secondary

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.610
structure into account.

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:37.460
These structural elements, stem
loops, come from the Rfam

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:41.830
database, and there are a few on
SARS-CoV-2 as well.

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:48.900
So, VADR -- I'm going to talk
about pretty specifically what

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.339
VADR does.

00:06:50.339 --> 00:06:53.860
So, I'm going to go through the
four stages of analysis that

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.649
VADR does.

00:06:55.649 --> 00:06:57.330
On this slide, I'm just going to
list them, and then I'm going to

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:01.449
go into detail for each of them
in the next slides.

00:07:01.449 --> 00:07:06.240
So, VADR annotates each sequence
using its best-matching model.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.089
So it's actually set up to use a
library of different RefSeqs and

00:07:10.089 --> 00:07:14.550
to select the closest one and
then use that RefSeq to do the

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:17.679
annotation for each incoming
sequence.

00:07:17.679 --> 00:07:21.110
For SARS-CoV-2, like I said, we
only have one RefSeq.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:24.689
So this classification is
essentially to make sure that

00:07:24.689 --> 00:07:27.409
the sequence is, in fact,
SARS-CoV-2.

00:07:27.409 --> 00:07:31.189
Once it determines the
best-matching model, it then

00:07:31.189 --> 00:07:35.789
moves on to a stage we call
coverage determination to find

00:07:35.789 --> 00:07:40.710
kind of the local hits against
the model and how they align.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:43.649
And then there's a global
alignment stage where it

00:07:43.649 --> 00:07:46.389
computes a global alignment of
the sequence of the model and

00:07:46.389 --> 00:07:49.599
then uses that to map the
features just using the

00:07:49.599 --> 00:07:53.739
coordinates of the features from
the RefSeq, just mapping them

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:55.779
based on the alignment.

00:07:55.779 --> 00:07:59.409
And then finally, we do a
protein validation stage, where

00:07:59.409 --> 00:08:03.999
we translate each CDS and then
compare it to the RefSeq

00:08:03.999 --> 00:08:05.649
proteins using BLASTX.

00:08:05.649 --> 00:08:08.800
And the reason we have these
four different stages, and I'll

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.599
go into this more in the next
slides, but is that each one of

00:08:12.599 --> 00:08:15.869
them identifies different types
of issues, or what we call

00:08:15.869 --> 00:08:18.239
alerts in the sequence.

00:08:18.239 --> 00:08:23.030
And these are going to alert the
indexer as to these unusual

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:25.580
features of the sequence that
they should look at.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:31.550
So, in the first stage, we have
a library of models,

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:35.849
potentially, where we compare
the sequence against each model

00:08:35.849 --> 00:08:37.089
in the library.

00:08:37.089 --> 00:08:40.550
In the case of SARS-CoV-2,
again, we only have one, but it

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:43.681
is set up -- The reason I
mention this here is because it

00:08:43.681 --> 00:08:46.810
is set up to use potentially
multiple references.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:51.420
So if we did add new references
based on common mutations in

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:54.990
SARS-CoV-2, you could
potentially differentiate

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.399
between those with VADR.

00:08:57.399 --> 00:09:01.139
So, we select the best-matching
one, and then that model is used

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:02.730
in all the subsequent steps.

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:08.629
And so, at this stage, I'm now
showing you a table that has a

00:09:08.629 --> 00:09:12.510
list of the types of alerts, or
issues, that we could find with

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:14.300
this sequence at this stage.

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.930
So, one of them is no
annotations.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:21.029
So, if it doesn't match any of
the models, that's an issue,

00:09:21.029 --> 00:09:22.129
that's a problem.

00:09:22.129 --> 00:09:24.600
Could be that it's
reverse-complimented because the

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:26.510
match is on the opposite strand.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:29.950
And with VADR, you can actually
specify what you think the

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:34.089
sequence is, and if it matches
to a different model -- You can

00:09:34.089 --> 00:09:36.709
specify the type of model you
think it is, like dengue, and if

00:09:36.709 --> 00:09:39.519
it matches to a norovirus, for
example, that would be a

00:09:39.519 --> 00:09:41.519
different type of problem.

00:09:41.519 --> 00:09:45.990
So, in the second stage, now
that we have the best-matching

00:09:45.990 --> 00:09:51.279
model, we use a BLASTN-like
algorithm to do local-local

00:09:51.279 --> 00:09:52.920
alignment between the model and
the sequence.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.730
So, we actually don't use
BLASTN.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:57.000
We use HMMER3.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.730
But it's the same type of idea.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:03.439
So, if we have -- I'm going to
go through some example

00:10:03.439 --> 00:10:04.439
scenarios.

00:10:04.439 --> 00:10:06.220
So, imagine this S1 sequence
here.

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:09.420
Let's say that's a full-length
sequence to the model.

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:13.100
We expect one hit that is
essentially the full length of

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:14.709
the model, and that's expected.

00:10:14.709 --> 00:10:16.990
So there's no alerts reported
there.

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:20.000
We could also have a shorter
sequence that's not full-length

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:23.819
with respect to the model, but
the entire sequence is covered

00:10:23.819 --> 00:10:25.300
by the match to the model.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:26.840
So that's just a partial
sequence.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:29.000
Again, there's no problem there.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:32.389
But we could have other
situations, like for this S3

00:10:32.389 --> 00:10:39.089
example, where the match does
not extend to the full length of

00:10:39.089 --> 00:10:40.089
the sequence.

00:10:40.089 --> 00:10:42.720
So there's extra sequence on
either "N" that doesn't match

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.459
the model because it's not
similar enough.

00:10:45.459 --> 00:10:48.290
And so, those are specific types
of alerts that we then report.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:51.960
And so, in this case, there's
low similarity in the 5-prime

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:54.389
"N," low similarity on the
3-prime "N," and also we'd have

00:10:54.389 --> 00:10:57.810
a low coverage alert because not
enough of the sequence matches

00:10:57.810 --> 00:10:59.279
the model.

00:10:59.279 --> 00:11:03.350
So, in another case, we might
have multiple hits, where

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:05.899
there's a region in the middle
that doesn't match the model.

00:11:05.899 --> 00:11:08.819
Again, that's a different type
of alert.

00:11:08.819 --> 00:11:13.190
So, at this stage, like I
mentioned, low coverage.

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:15.550
We could still have other
problems that we identify.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:19.990
We could have the case where two
parts of the sequence match to

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:21.769
the same part of the model.

00:11:21.769 --> 00:11:25.920
So that's a duplicate regions
alert.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:30.970
We could have a case where the
order of the hits with respect

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:34.540
to the model is different than
the order with respect to the

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:35.540
sequence.

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:39.269
So you have kind of the sequence
in the wrong order.

00:11:39.269 --> 00:11:41.350
That's a different type of
alert.

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:42.970
We could have hits on both
strands.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:47.079
I mentioned these low-similarity
issues with the previous

00:11:47.079 --> 00:11:48.290
examples.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:53.350
So, then, on the next stage, we
do another comparison with the

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:57.860
model, and this time we do a
global alignment to the model,

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:00.800
where we just take the sequence,
so, going back to the S1

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:04.029
example, and we align it to the
full model.

00:12:04.029 --> 00:12:06.300
And based on that alignment, we
can just map all of these

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:10.350
features and get the "N" points
of all the features very simply.

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:13.699
And again, S1 and S2, we would
have no problems with their

00:12:13.699 --> 00:12:17.050
perfectly good sequences that we
don't see anything wrong with.

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:23.370
Now, S3, I showed you on the
previous example, we had extra

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:24.939
sequence that didn't match.

00:12:24.939 --> 00:12:28.399
When we do a global alignment,
we're actually forcing it to

00:12:28.399 --> 00:12:29.610
align at those N's.

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:34.389
So, I bring this up to point out
that that's why I also do that

00:12:34.389 --> 00:12:39.110
other BLASTN/HMMER3 stage
because if we just forced a

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:41.889
global alignment, it would be
more difficult to tell that

00:12:41.889 --> 00:12:43.740
there was anything wrong with
these N's.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:47.810
So, we do both a full alignment
and the coverage determination

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:50.270
step to kind of identify
different types of problems.

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:53.139
Again, with S4, this region
would be aligned.

00:12:53.139 --> 00:12:55.740
So, it would be harder to tell
there was something wrong there

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:59.319
without first processing the
alignment.

00:12:59.319 --> 00:13:01.439
But we could have other
situations that we do find

00:13:01.439 --> 00:13:03.519
problems with now that we're
doing a full alignment.

00:13:03.519 --> 00:13:09.439
So, this S5 example, we could
find that when we try to map

00:13:09.439 --> 00:13:14.240
this mat-peptide boundary here,
we find that it's a gap in the

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:15.240
alignment.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:18.779
And so, we basically are saying
we're not sure where that

00:13:18.779 --> 00:13:21.410
position should be for the end
of that mat-peptide.

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:24.199
And so, that's a different type
of alert.

00:13:24.199 --> 00:13:28.660
We could also have a case where
we have low confidence in those

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:32.110
"N" points, so the co-variance
model's a probabilistic model,

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:37.350
and we're able to compute the
posterior probability that any

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:41.009
nucleotide is aligned to any
position in the model.

00:13:41.009 --> 00:13:46.920
And that acts as a confidence
estimate into how well resolved

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.699
the alignment is there, kind of
how certain the model is that

00:13:49.699 --> 00:13:51.439
the alignment is correct.

00:13:51.439 --> 00:13:54.670
And if that probability is low
enough, it's basically telling

00:13:54.670 --> 00:13:58.029
us that it's uncertain where
that boundary should go.

00:13:58.029 --> 00:14:01.279
So that's a different type of
alert.

00:14:01.279 --> 00:14:04.889
We also translate coding regions
at this stage and look for

00:14:04.889 --> 00:14:06.430
in-frame early stops.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:08.079
So there could be an in-frame
early stop.

00:14:08.079 --> 00:14:11.420
That's a different type of
problem that we'll detect.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:16.529
So, at the alignment stage,
there's a lot more alerts we can

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:18.529
detect.

00:14:18.529 --> 00:14:21.120
Most of them have to do with
coding potential, so mutation in

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:25.759
the start codon, mutation in the
stop codon, early stops.

00:14:25.759 --> 00:14:28.449
We could also have mature
peptides that aren't adjacent to

00:14:28.449 --> 00:14:30.399
each other.

00:14:30.399 --> 00:14:35.069
And I mentioned these indefinite
annotations.

00:14:35.069 --> 00:14:38.550
That's when there's a gap or a
low confidence at the end of a

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:39.550
feature.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:44.730
So, in the final stage, we do
protein validation.

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:48.970
And like I mentioned before, we
just translate each CDS and then

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:54.189
blast it using BLASTX against
the RefSeq proteins, and then we

00:14:54.189 --> 00:15:00.019
expect that BLASTX alignment to
be full-length with respect to

00:15:00.019 --> 00:15:02.980
the CDS -- predicted CDS.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:07.990
And so, in this case, if we had
an early stop or a frame-shift

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:11.379
mutation, we would get a shorter
alignment, and that's a specific

00:15:11.379 --> 00:15:15.670
type of alert that we report at
that stage.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:20.860
I wanted to mention Alejandro
Shaffer is a colleague of mine

00:15:20.860 --> 00:15:22.720
that implemented this stage.

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:29.519
So, at this point -- Sorry, in
the protein validation stage, we

00:15:29.519 --> 00:15:30.839
identified these early stops.

00:15:30.839 --> 00:15:36.110
We also report an error if the
blast alignment doesn't extend

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:37.870
to the end.

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:42.209
And also, we look for very large
insertions or deletions at this

00:15:42.209 --> 00:15:45.839
stage, and that's a different
type of alert that's reported.

00:15:45.839 --> 00:15:51.360
So, that's how -- Those are the
four stages of VADR, and that's

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.829
how VADR works in general.

00:15:54.829 --> 00:15:57.459
And those are the alerts that we
develop when we're testing for

00:15:57.459 --> 00:15:59.889
norovirus and dengue virus.

00:15:59.889 --> 00:16:03.519
When we started to analyze
SARS-CoV-2, we realized that

00:16:03.519 --> 00:16:06.880
there were two issues that we
had to address specifically for

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:07.880
SARS-CoV-2.

00:16:07.880 --> 00:16:12.110
So, the first one is the
processing time.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:16.619
So, the co-variants model
alignment, I mentioned that it

00:16:16.619 --> 00:16:20.579
incorporates secondary structure
into the alignment.

00:16:20.579 --> 00:16:23.620
That comes at a computational
cost that's a more expensive

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.939
algorithm than just
sequence-only alignment.

00:16:25.939 --> 00:16:30.230
And for SARS-CoV-2, it's so
long, and that algorithm scales

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:34.120
with more than the cube of the
length of the sequence.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:37.350
So it's especially slow for very
long sequences.

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:39.970
For SARS-CoV-2, it was taking
about five minutes for a

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:41.170
full-length sequence.

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:44.910
So, that was way too slow for
high throughput.

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:48.810
So we implemented a heuristic
based on BLASTN, kind of

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:52.040
first-pass alignment that
reduces that to about two

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:54.249
seconds per typical sequence.

00:16:54.249 --> 00:16:59.800
So now it's not blazingly fast
by any means, but it's fast

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:02.160
enough for kind of the
throughput that we're using it

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.459
for for GenBank submissions.

00:17:04.459 --> 00:17:13.339
So, the second issue is that
SARS-CoV-2 submissions --

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:16.150
SARS-CoV-2 sequences are being
submitted tended to have a lot

00:17:16.150 --> 00:17:21.990
of ambiguous ends in them --
stretches of ends in them.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:24.559
And if you look at this table
down here, it's showing you the

00:17:24.559 --> 00:17:28.640
percentage of nucleotides that
are N's in SARS-CoV-2,

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.450
norovirus, and dengue virus.

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:33.480
And more importantly, the
percentage of sequences that

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:35.890
have at least one stretch that's
fairly long.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:38.100
So, in this case, greater than
50 nucleotides.

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:41.279
And you see about a quarter of
the sequences have a stretch of

00:17:41.279 --> 00:17:42.279
N's greater than 50 nucleotides.

00:17:42.279 --> 00:17:48.750
The reason that's so important
is because VADR and other

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:52.010
annotation tools rely on
sequence similarity.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:54.799
And so, whenever there's a
stretch of N's, it essentially

00:17:54.799 --> 00:17:57.980
can't get purchase on that part
of the alignment.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:00.580
It has to rely on the bordering
sequences.

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:04.559
And so, it just makes it more
difficult to do the alignment

00:18:04.559 --> 00:18:05.559
correctly.

00:18:05.559 --> 00:18:09.320
So, what we implemented was a
method to identify those

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:14.350
stretches of N's, identify where
they map to in the RefSeq --

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:17.860
again, based on a first-pass
BLASTN alignment -- and then

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:21.630
replace those N's with the
expected nucleotides just taking

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:24.030
them directly from the RefSeq.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:28.280
Then we do the full analysis
using those sequences with those

00:18:28.280 --> 00:18:29.280
N's replaced.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:32.370
And that seems to work pretty
well.

00:18:32.370 --> 00:18:39.570
So, what I'm showing you now is
the observed counts of the

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:43.980
different types of problems that
VADR is identifying in

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:45.309
SARS-CoV-2 sequences.

00:18:45.309 --> 00:18:47.600
So, I just want to make two
points.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:49.669
I'm not going to go through all
of them.

00:18:49.669 --> 00:18:52.580
But the two points I want to
make are there's many different

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:54.230
types of problems.

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:58.679
So, it's finding a lot of
different issues.

00:18:58.679 --> 00:19:02.020
And some of them are much more
common than others.

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:05.330
So, that duplicate regions is a
pretty egregious problem, but

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:07.250
it doesn't happen very often.

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:12.030
The more common ones are like
CDS with an early stop codon,

00:19:12.030 --> 00:19:15.840
for example, or other problems
with the BLASTX alignment not

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.649
extending far enough.

00:19:18.649 --> 00:19:24.450
So, now I'm just going to go
through just two examples of

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:30.670
real SARS-CoV-2 sequences and
alerts that VADR reported on

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:31.670
them.

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:34.150
So, this first one was actually
fairly early.

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:37.880
This was, I believe, a sequence
from February.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:44.730
And this had this error,
duplicate regions, where it

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:47.371
identified that there was one
part of the sequence that

00:19:47.371 --> 00:19:49.740
actually maps to the same part
of the model.

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:51.860
Or sorry, two parts of the
sequence that map to the same

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:52.860
part of the model.

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:54.340
And this is the alert.

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:56.700
This is the message that VADR
reports.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:20:00.060
It tells you the coordinates of
where those -- So, this is the

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:01.840
region that overlaps.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:05.530
And it tells you that
information so you can then go

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:06.530
and look at it.

00:20:06.530 --> 00:20:08.810
It also identified that at the
beginning, there was a stretch

00:20:08.810 --> 00:20:12.399
of 24 nucleotides that didn't
match to the model.

00:20:12.399 --> 00:20:14.769
And again, at the end, there was
a stretch that didn't match the

00:20:14.769 --> 00:20:15.769
model.

00:20:15.769 --> 00:20:19.070
And so, now I'm going to show
you an alignment of those

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.070
regions.

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.070
&gt;&gt; Eric?

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:22.070
&gt;&gt; Yes?

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:23.070
&gt;&gt; This is Ryan.

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.470
Lee Katz in the chat asked a
question that I think you could

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:29.390
clarify quickly, which is just
when you're substituting the

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:33.390
RefSeq sequence for the N's of
the submitted sequence, that's

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:36.950
just for the purpose of the QA
step.

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:40.059
That's not making it into the
GenBank record, assuming it gets

00:20:40.059 --> 00:20:41.360
accepted, correct?

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:42.660
&gt;&gt; Correct, yeah.

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:46.269
I should have been more clear
about that.

00:20:46.269 --> 00:20:50.560
Yes, so, we replace the N's with
the RefSeq nucleotides, do the

00:20:50.560 --> 00:20:55.160
full analysis, and then put them
back, basically, before it goes

00:20:55.160 --> 00:20:57.840
into GenBank.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:01.210
Okay, so --
&gt;&gt; I apologize, Eric.

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:02.960
Can I follow up on that?

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:04.450
&gt;&gt; No, go ahead.

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:05.940
&gt;&gt; This is Lee.

00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:06.940
&gt;&gt; That's fine.

00:21:06.940 --> 00:21:08.700
&gt;&gt; I just ran VADR for the first
time this morning.

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:12.080
So this is fresh on my mind, and
it's great.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:13.080
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:16.700
&gt;&gt; And so, when I ran it, it
produced that table file, which

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:19.640
means I could take my genome
assembly and upload that to

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:22.090
GenBank with those annotations.

00:21:22.090 --> 00:21:27.710
Is that possible that those
artificial annotations could

00:21:27.710 --> 00:21:31.820
make their way to GenBank just
by what my work flow was?

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:37.890
&gt;&gt; So, you're talking about the
boundaries of the annotation.

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:41.760
So, do you mean, like, if you
had a stretch of N's at the end

00:21:41.760 --> 00:21:47.390
of a CDS, that the boundary
would be an "N"?

00:21:47.390 --> 00:21:49.240
Is that what you mean?

00:21:49.240 --> 00:21:53.299
&gt;&gt; So, like, the boundary of the
gene might be artificially kind

00:21:53.299 --> 00:21:57.620
of guessed on that QA/QC step,
but that might also make it into

00:21:57.620 --> 00:22:03.450
the table file, which I would
then upload to GenBank.

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:08.350
&gt;&gt; So, there's rules in GenBank
that, for example, CDS cannot

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:11.000
end with an "N."

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:14.880
So, those positions get what we
call trimmed.

00:22:14.880 --> 00:22:20.640
So they get modified to the
3 prime most -- like, if it was

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:25.559
at the end of the CDS, the 3
prime most non-N, and then we

00:22:25.559 --> 00:22:30.669
add this, like, "greater-than"
sign that indicates that it's

00:22:30.669 --> 00:22:36.240
not to the end -- it's partial,
basically.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:37.840
So I don't know if that answers
your question.

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:42.380
So, VADR should generate the
annotations, like, with that

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:45.590
greater-than sign, instead of
saying that it's a full-length

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:46.590
CDS.

00:22:46.590 --> 00:22:48.559
Does that answer your question?

00:22:48.559 --> 00:22:49.559
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:22:49.559 --> 00:22:54.440
I think I'm understanding that I
might have some -- It's possible

00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:57.850
I have some noise in the table
file locally, but the GenBank

00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:00.659
submission process would catch
that.

00:23:00.659 --> 00:23:01.929
&gt;&gt; I believe that's right.

00:23:01.929 --> 00:23:05.960
so, there's additional steps
that are done to go from the

00:23:05.960 --> 00:23:09.649
VADR output to the final files
that go into GenBank.

00:23:09.649 --> 00:23:12.480
Linda Yankie can speak better to
it than I can.

00:23:12.480 --> 00:23:16.490
So maybe we could bring that up
with her.

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:21.769
And also, you can e-mail us and
give us a specific example, and

00:23:21.769 --> 00:23:24.050
we can look at it also.

00:23:24.050 --> 00:23:27.730
&gt;&gt; Thanks, I appreciate it.

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:30.490
&gt;&gt; Sure, no problem.

00:23:30.490 --> 00:23:34.510
Okay, so, for this first
example, I'm now just showing

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:37.481
you an alignment of what that
looks like in the actual

00:23:37.481 --> 00:23:38.500
sequence data.

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:42.110
So, at the 5 prime end, there
was a problem where this

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:48.149
sequence was not similar enough,
and that actually matches to the

00:23:48.149 --> 00:23:49.149
end of the RefSeq.

00:23:49.149 --> 00:23:52.280
So there was some problem with,
perhaps, the assembly of that

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:53.280
sequence.

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:55.940
And again, at the end -- so,
this is now the 3 prime end --

00:23:55.940 --> 00:24:02.360
this region matched best to the
middle of the RefSeq.

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:05.130
And that was actually reported
by VADR, that it identified that

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:06.850
that region was duplicated.

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:08.690
So, that's one example.

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:13.419
In that case, the sequence was
trimmed by Linda, and it then

00:24:13.419 --> 00:24:14.419
went in.

00:24:14.419 --> 00:24:16.460
So, those N's were trimmed
because they were deemed to be

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.070
not accurate.

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:23.240
So, for the second example, this
is a more recent sequence.

00:24:23.240 --> 00:24:26.400
And in this case --
&gt;&gt; Eric?

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:27.549
&gt;&gt; Yes?

00:24:27.549 --> 00:24:32.409
&gt;&gt; So, going back to the
previous one, the low similarity

00:24:32.409 --> 00:24:36.600
-- and this is Pavitra from
University of Washington.

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:42.220
Does it also call an error for
low similarity and if there's a

00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:45.350
differing number of A's in that
poly-A at the end?

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:46.880
How sensitive is that?

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:52.679
Because I've seen a lot of ours
get called out for that reason.

00:24:52.679 --> 00:24:57.600
&gt;&gt; Right, so, that's something
we've talked about.

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:03.580
So, the end -- Yes, it doesn't
treat that end very differently

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:08.669
than any other region of the
alignment, although it could.

00:25:08.669 --> 00:25:14.920
So, if it doesn't match well in
this poly-A track, it could

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:16.880
potentially report an error in
that.

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:19.480
If that's something that's
happening a lot and it's

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:24.010
biologically valid, that's
something we can look at to try

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:26.289
to, like, come up with a way of
dealing with that specialty.

00:25:26.289 --> 00:25:28.669
So, if you have a lot of
examples of those that you want

00:25:28.669 --> 00:25:31.179
us to look at, we can.

00:25:31.179 --> 00:25:35.919
&gt;&gt; I just feel like we shouldn't
penalize just for having, like,

00:25:35.919 --> 00:25:41.779
three A's at the end, versus the
exact number as the RefSeq

00:25:41.779 --> 00:25:45.670
because I guess, you know, like,
your assembly might just

00:25:45.670 --> 00:25:50.120
generate those many, and we try
not to introduce, like,

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:54.100
additional things from the
reference, for obvious reasons.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:56.639
But that's just what the
assembly came up with.

00:25:56.639 --> 00:25:57.979
&gt;&gt; Right, yeah, no.

00:25:57.979 --> 00:26:00.100
I think I understand.

00:26:00.100 --> 00:26:03.909
It's just, like, because it's
treating that the same as any

00:26:03.909 --> 00:26:07.679
other part, it's going to throw
an alert, but like I said, I

00:26:07.679 --> 00:26:09.789
agree with you, and it probably
shouldn't.

00:26:09.789 --> 00:26:12.450
So, if you have examples of
that and you could help us out

00:26:12.450 --> 00:26:15.900
by sending them, we'd be happy
to look at that.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:16.900
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:26:16.900 --> 00:26:18.220
Will do.

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:19.220
Thanks.

00:26:19.220 --> 00:26:25.299
&gt;&gt; Okay, so, for this second
example, it was just an early

00:26:25.299 --> 00:26:26.299
in-frame stop.

00:26:26.299 --> 00:26:31.850
So, this is in ORF8, I believe,
which commonly has issues like

00:26:31.850 --> 00:26:39.169
this, where there was a stop --
342 nucleotides, 5 prime of

00:26:39.169 --> 00:26:42.830
where it was expected to be,
based on alignment to the

00:26:42.830 --> 00:26:43.830
RefSeq.

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:46.340
So it was an early stop, and
this was detected twice, both in

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:50.390
the alignment stage, and then
again in the protein validation

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:51.390
stage.

00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:52.899
But they're both saying the same
thing, although it's two

00:26:52.899 --> 00:26:54.970
separate alerts.

00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:57.669
And then if we look at the
sequence, this is an alignment

00:26:57.669 --> 00:26:58.850
of ORF8.

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:00.440
It's part of the alignment.

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:01.820
It starts at these positions.

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:07.580
This sequence starts in the
second nucleotide of the RefSeq.

00:27:07.580 --> 00:27:10.690
So these numbers are off by one.

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:13.850
But it's completely identical,
except for this one mutation

00:27:13.850 --> 00:27:14.850
here.

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:19.610
And it's changed the GDA in the
RefSeq to a TGA.

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:23.450
Yeah, so, it's introducing the
stop, and that's what it's

00:27:23.450 --> 00:27:24.450
detecting.

00:27:24.450 --> 00:27:29.130
In this case, Linda went back to
the submitter, who kind of

00:27:29.130 --> 00:27:34.120
verified that it was correct,
and this sequence went in as it

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:35.120
was.

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:38.029
So this is an example of a VADR
problem that turned out to go

00:27:38.029 --> 00:27:40.910
into GenBank because it wasn't
actually a problem.

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:46.320
Okay, so, I'm going to wrap up
in the next couple slides.

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:50.559
So, I just wanted to mention
some limitations of VADR.

00:27:50.559 --> 00:27:54.799
So, one of them is it works in
nucleotide space.

00:27:54.799 --> 00:27:57.549
We did that mainly so it could
do global alignment of the full

00:27:57.549 --> 00:28:00.179
genomes.

00:28:00.179 --> 00:28:03.190
But as many of you know, if you
work in protein space, you have

00:28:03.190 --> 00:28:06.610
more statistical power because
of the larger alphabet.

00:28:06.610 --> 00:28:12.880
And we're not working in protein
space, except for the BLASTX

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:14.110
step.

00:28:14.110 --> 00:28:19.539
So that's a limitation.

00:28:19.539 --> 00:28:23.080
Your RefSeq or your model really
has to be representative of the

00:28:23.080 --> 00:28:24.080
sequences you're trying to
annotate.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:28.320
If you have divergent sequences
that have novel stops, like that

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:31.250
earlier example, they're going
to fail.

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:34.210
So you really want to make sure
you have kind of coverage in

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:40.830
your model of everything you're
expecting to allow as valid.

00:28:40.830 --> 00:28:42.039
This is a practical one.

00:28:42.039 --> 00:28:45.490
So, I mention the slow run time
of covariance model alignment.

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:47.160
It's also very memory-intensive.

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:53.539
So, that heuristic that we
implemented to get the speed

00:28:53.539 --> 00:28:57.610
down to two seconds also reduces
memory usage, but in some cases

00:28:57.610 --> 00:29:08.720
-- in rare cases, like 1 in
1,000 sequences empirically,

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:13.450
that doesn't really work, and
so, you have to fall back to the

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:15.240
expensive CM alignment.

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:21.480
So you do need 64 gigabytes of
RAM to run VADR for SARS-CoV-2.

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:22.610
And that's a big limitation.

00:29:22.610 --> 00:29:25.480
We realize that.

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:30.020
I wanted to point out some
important features.

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:32.529
So, like I mentioned before,
this pass/fail decision is

00:29:32.529 --> 00:29:37.259
really important to us, and the
indexers or users have precise

00:29:37.259 --> 00:29:38.259
control over that.

00:29:38.259 --> 00:29:42.009
So you can change which of those
alerts are fatal versus

00:29:42.009 --> 00:29:45.350
non-fatal, so it caused the
sequence to fail or not, as well

00:29:45.350 --> 00:29:48.169
as the thresholds for many of
them.

00:29:48.169 --> 00:29:52.710
So, VADR is on GitHub.

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:56.030
There's a standalone version you
can download and use for

00:29:56.030 --> 00:29:58.730
submitters and also for other
purposes.

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:02.200
So, if you're just interested in
doing analysis of SARS-CoV-2

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:06.130
without submitting the
sequences, there is a separate,

00:30:06.130 --> 00:30:10.720
we think, potential pool of
users that would use it for that

00:30:10.720 --> 00:30:12.510
case, and also other viruses.

00:30:12.510 --> 00:30:16.790
You can build your own models
and use it for them.

00:30:16.790 --> 00:30:21.700
It's a general tool, but again,
you really need the models to

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:24.779
cover all the diversity that
you're expecting.

00:30:24.779 --> 00:30:30.559
And the way that the alignment
feature mapping works is really

00:30:30.559 --> 00:30:31.559
flexible.

00:30:31.559 --> 00:30:35.010
So you can have a feature as
short as a single nucleotide in

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:37.600
potentially multiple segments,
and that's what allows us to

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:39.941
handle things like program frame
shifts, where you have a

00:30:39.941 --> 00:30:46.149
duplicated nucleotide in a CDS
very easily.

00:30:46.149 --> 00:30:51.330
So, just want to mention there
is documentation on GitHub.

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:54.309
I made a special page just for
SARS-CoV-2 analysis.

00:30:54.309 --> 00:31:00.220
It's kind of a tutorial of how
to do this, or how to use VADR

00:31:00.220 --> 00:31:01.769
for SARS-CoV-2.

00:31:01.769 --> 00:31:05.309
And then in the source code,
there's mark-down files.

00:31:05.309 --> 00:31:09.220
So, v-annotate is the script
used to do the annotation.

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:11.440
And it has all the command-line
options and everything

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:13.159
documented.

00:31:13.159 --> 00:31:17.500
Okay, and so, I think I'm going
to pause now and see if there's

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:22.659
any other questions, since I
can't look at the chat.

