00:34:42.620 --> 00:34:46.970
Okay, if there's no other
questions, I will hand it off to

00:34:46.970 --> 00:34:49.720
Linda Yankie, who's going to
speak next.

00:34:49.720 --> 00:34:56.740
&gt;&gt; So, just a quick recap of
what VADR, and how we use it for

00:34:56.740 --> 00:34:57.740
submissions.

00:34:57.740 --> 00:34:59.870
First and foremost is speed.

00:34:59.870 --> 00:35:04.690
Every submitter will tell you
that the hardest part of

00:35:04.690 --> 00:35:09.100
submitting data to GenBank for
them is adding the annotations.

00:35:09.100 --> 00:35:12.330
And by providing this tool that
not only you can check your data

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:15.340
before you submit, but also,
we'll run the tool for you and

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:17.110
add the annotation for you.

00:35:17.110 --> 00:35:20.890
It saves you a lot of time, and
it also results in data being

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:23.860
released to the database much
faster.

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:28.141
The quality control aspect --
VADR checks the sequence and

00:35:28.141 --> 00:35:30.910
does the coding and non-coding
regions.

00:35:30.910 --> 00:35:35.200
And if you've looked at a lot of
DNA analysis, you know sometimes

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:38.910
catching anomalies in non-coding
regions is harder to do.

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:42.400
The consistency -- providing the
standard nomenclature for both

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:44.680
the genes and the products.

00:35:44.680 --> 00:35:49.420
That's actually an extremely
important aspect for data

00:35:49.420 --> 00:35:51.470
retrieval and doing comparison
studies.

00:35:51.470 --> 00:35:54.610
And also, there's value added.

00:35:54.610 --> 00:35:57.930
Most submissions that come to
us, even if viruses don't

00:35:57.930 --> 00:36:02.150
contain all of the mature
peptides for the RNA structural

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:05.430
components, but VADR actually
will add for it.

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:08.740
And note that it does do the
mature peptides on both of the

00:36:08.740 --> 00:36:12.690
polyproteins in SARS, which can
be a little tricky, since

00:36:12.690 --> 00:36:13.990
they're duplicated.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:16.590
Next slide, please, Eric.

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:17.590
Eric?

00:36:17.590 --> 00:36:19.180
&gt;&gt; Sorry about that.

00:36:19.180 --> 00:36:20.480
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:21.780
No problem.

00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:23.760
So, we do have two mechanisms to
submit.

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:27.070
And we've been talking a lot,
and I'm going to change this up

00:36:27.070 --> 00:36:30.030
a little bit because of the
questions that were asked

00:36:30.030 --> 00:36:31.030
previously.

00:36:31.030 --> 00:36:34.880
And both of the submission
protocols that we have right

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:41.670
now, the common requirement of
FASTA, the source metadata in a

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:44.530
table format, and submitter
information.

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:47.730
you do not need to run table to
ASN yourself.

00:36:47.730 --> 00:36:51.420
You do not need to supply that
feature table that's output for

00:36:51.420 --> 00:36:52.420
VADR.

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:55.130
Again, VADR's great if you want
to check before you send to us,

00:36:55.130 --> 00:36:58.100
but you do not need to supply
that to us.

00:36:58.100 --> 00:37:04.210
We will run table to ASN and run
VADR for you.

00:37:04.210 --> 00:37:07.630
We have both a web-based
interface and a programmatic

00:37:07.630 --> 00:37:08.930
interface.

00:37:08.930 --> 00:37:13.521
I believe the last time we
spoke, we talked about the

00:37:13.521 --> 00:37:15.910
web-based because programmatic
wasn't available yet.

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:22.150
They're both up and running, and
in both cases, you can supply

00:37:22.150 --> 00:37:25.930
the BioProject, the BioSample,
and the SRA run accession, or

00:37:25.930 --> 00:37:29.490
the SRR, so to speak, in the
source table, and it will be

00:37:29.490 --> 00:37:31.410
picked up and added to your
records.

00:37:31.410 --> 00:37:34.640
Provide that link between all of
your data.

00:37:34.640 --> 00:37:38.870
While the way that you submit
the data is different, the

00:37:38.870 --> 00:37:40.050
reporting system is the same.

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:44.110
So, for both of them, we gather
the files that are supplied, run

00:37:44.110 --> 00:37:48.930
VADR to get the feature table,
run table to ASN, and then

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:53.830
output either the error report
or any kind of accession

00:37:53.830 --> 00:37:57.610
reports, all of that to the web
for you.

00:37:57.610 --> 00:38:00.280
Next slide, please.

00:38:00.280 --> 00:38:03.410
So, I'm going to talk a little
bit about the web-based

00:38:03.410 --> 00:38:06.070
submission first because that's
what we seem to be getting the

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:07.810
most of right now.

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:12.850
Basically, it's forms online
that prompt you for the required

00:38:12.850 --> 00:38:13.850
information.

00:38:13.850 --> 00:38:17.260
The source information that you
supply can be a table that you

00:38:17.260 --> 00:38:20.910
already have and you import, or
actually, within the program,

00:38:20.910 --> 00:38:25.670
there is an editable table so
that you can copy, paste, move

00:38:25.670 --> 00:38:28.120
things around, and so forth as
you go.

00:38:28.120 --> 00:38:34.080
A big advantage, in my opinion,
of using the web-based form is,

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:37.020
particularly for smaller groups
of submissions, is that there's

00:38:37.020 --> 00:38:41.870
real-time validation of certain
things like the country, date,

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:47.510
isolate format or sequence
length and vector screening.

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:50.840
You can see this is actually a
snippet of one from influenza.

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:53.350
That's why the error is about
ceratose.

00:38:53.350 --> 00:38:57.850
But the general idea is you'll
get a warning or an error if

00:38:57.850 --> 00:39:01.110
something is wrong right away,
while you're actually in the

00:39:01.110 --> 00:39:04.430
program, so that you can make
any changes you need to make at

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:05.580
the time.

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:07.470
Errors need to be fixed before
you can move on.

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:10.890
Warnings are for things like,
"Hey, you only put the

00:39:10.890 --> 00:39:13.740
collection date year, and it
would be really nice to have

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:14.740
more than that."

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:18.520
So that way we can try and get
as much information as we can.

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:22.560
Next slide, please, Eric.

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:28.450
So, the programmatic submissions
are if any of you -- and I know

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:32.760
there are people on this call
who have used the same system to

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:35.940
submit influenza sequences in
the past.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:40.950
Essentially, you upload a .zip
archive and a submission.xml

00:39:40.950 --> 00:39:42.270
file via FTP.

00:39:42.270 --> 00:39:45.220
The archive contains that .fsa,
the source.

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:48.040
.sbt is the submitter
information.

00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:52.580
And you can also supply an
optional comment field, a file

00:39:52.580 --> 00:39:55.800
that will show up in the comment
field in the final flat file in

00:39:55.800 --> 00:39:58.390
the programmatic submissions.

00:39:58.390 --> 00:40:01.000
You do need to include that
submission.xml file.

00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:07.230
It's basically the directions
that tell us where this data is

00:40:07.230 --> 00:40:09.590
supposed to go and what pipeline
to use.

00:40:09.590 --> 00:40:13.290
Again, there's some basic
sequence checks that are done as

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:20.050
soon as we receive the files --
the low-quality vector sequence

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:22.250
links, the format.

00:40:22.250 --> 00:40:26.190
If all of that and all the files
are there, essentially, you get

00:40:26.190 --> 00:40:30.850
a temporary SUB number the same
way you do if you use the

00:40:30.850 --> 00:40:32.470
web-based form.

00:40:32.470 --> 00:40:37.270
And that will be assigned and
available in the report.xml.

00:40:37.270 --> 00:40:41.750
Then we'll go through again, run
VADR, run table to ASN, and any

00:40:41.750 --> 00:40:45.760
accessions or files will be
posted on Submission Portal for

00:40:45.760 --> 00:40:48.460
these submissions, as well.

00:40:48.460 --> 00:40:51.290
Next slide, please, Eric.

00:40:51.290 --> 00:40:55.760
So, what I have here is what is
two successful submissions.

00:40:55.760 --> 00:41:00.710
This was in our test system, so
all of these are made up.

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:04.050
The first one is using the
web-based pathway, and the

00:41:04.050 --> 00:41:06.630
second one is using the
programmatic interface.

00:41:06.630 --> 00:41:09.990
You'll notice that the output is
basically the same.

00:41:09.990 --> 00:41:12.130
You get the accession numbers.

00:41:12.130 --> 00:41:15.110
You get a tabulated file that
you can download that has the

00:41:15.110 --> 00:41:19.430
relationship between the session
number and the Seq ID, or

00:41:19.430 --> 00:41:23.440
sequence identifier used in your
original submissions.

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:27.810
You can also copy the flat file
and the e-mail that we send to

00:41:27.810 --> 00:41:29.890
you when it is released.

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:33.590
If you did not put a hold date
on your sequences, which I hope

00:41:33.590 --> 00:41:37.690
most of you are not, this will
be a live link that will take

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:43.910
you to the records in the
database as soon as they appear.

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:44.910
Next slide.

00:41:44.910 --> 00:41:49.570
If there is a VADR alert, you'll
get an error report.

00:41:49.570 --> 00:41:54.370
Again, the top one is a
web-based form, and the lower

00:41:54.370 --> 00:41:56.780
one is a programmatic.

00:41:56.780 --> 00:42:01.160
And you can see that there's a
link to an error report.

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:06.800
Again, it says "error," not
"alert."

00:42:06.800 --> 00:42:10.680
An error for us just means that
someone needs to look at it.

00:42:10.680 --> 00:42:15.990
So, if you were to download that
report and open it, you'll see

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:20.980
this, which is a list of the
errors as they appear and the

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:23.660
sequence identifiers for each
error.

00:42:23.660 --> 00:42:27.530
If you click on that link, it's
basically just an explanation of

00:42:27.530 --> 00:42:28.530
the error.

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:33.570
In some cases, we can give you
hints as to how to correct it,

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:37.880
not necessarily put a stop code
on, but for other errors, we

00:42:37.880 --> 00:42:38.880
can.

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:42.940
If you'll notice the submissions
that came in with the web-based

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:45.690
tool have a "fix" button.

00:42:45.690 --> 00:42:49.610
If you click on that button, it
will open up your previous

00:42:49.610 --> 00:42:54.320
submissions at the point where
the error was detected.

00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:57.950
So, for example, if there's a
problem with a stop code on,

00:42:57.950 --> 00:43:00.740
it's going to take you back to
the page where you uploaded

00:43:00.740 --> 00:43:06.390
sequences and give you a chance
to upload an amended file of

00:43:06.390 --> 00:43:09.800
sequences that corrects that
error.

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:14.201
Because the programmatic
interface doesn't have a web

00:43:14.201 --> 00:43:16.240
submission, we can't offer that.

00:43:16.240 --> 00:43:19.690
In order to fix those
submissions, you will have to

00:43:19.690 --> 00:43:26.970
re-upload the archive and the
submission of that xml file.

00:43:26.970 --> 00:43:28.670
Next slide, Eric.

00:43:28.670 --> 00:43:30.370
So, future improvements.

00:43:30.370 --> 00:43:32.640
What we were discussing.

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:36.440
Just as Eric made changes in
VADR to handle the N's, we've

00:43:36.440 --> 00:43:40.690
been making changes in the
validator, as well, and this

00:43:40.690 --> 00:43:44.920
will result in less sequences
being flagged for manual review,

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:49.010
and more will immediately flow
through to be released to the

00:43:49.010 --> 00:43:50.010
database.

00:43:50.010 --> 00:43:52.640
We also want to include another
error report.

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:56.340
So we'll still have that basic,
simple one, but we'll have the

00:43:56.340 --> 00:44:00.760
more detailed alt file that is
produced by VADR.

00:44:00.760 --> 00:44:03.850
And little snippets of the one
that is generated for the

00:44:03.850 --> 00:44:08.030
example -- this is the same
sequence that I showed the

00:44:08.030 --> 00:44:10.000
current error report for.

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:13.600
But you can see it says, "CDS
has stop code," just like

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:16.950
before, but now you at least
find out which protein has the

00:44:16.950 --> 00:44:17.950
stop code on.

00:44:17.950 --> 00:44:22.560
And it has a lot more details in
cases where there's a difference

00:44:22.560 --> 00:44:23.560
in the alignment.

00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:27.060
The actual nucleotide positions
are shown.

00:44:27.060 --> 00:44:30.480
And this will be something that
you can download and parse out

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:35.360
and use to QC the files
yourself.

00:44:35.360 --> 00:44:38.900
Okay, next slide, please.

00:44:38.900 --> 00:44:45.860
So, this is, as we were saying
earlier, VADR is going to alert

00:44:45.860 --> 00:44:49.330
on detected frame shifts or stop
codons.

00:44:49.330 --> 00:44:51.580
We know viruses mutate.

00:44:51.580 --> 00:44:54.530
We know that a lot of these are
actually correct.

00:44:54.530 --> 00:44:59.730
We've had a number of confirmed
mutations so far in the genes

00:44:59.730 --> 00:45:03.650
that are listed at the bottom of
the slide there.

00:45:03.650 --> 00:45:08.040
As Eric kept saying, we can add
VADR reference sequences.

00:45:08.040 --> 00:45:13.750
So, if you know of sequences
that have a mutation out of

00:45:13.750 --> 00:45:18.570
place that is consistent -- So,
let's say ORF7a is always

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:22.220
truncated at amino acid 50.

00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:27.210
Please let us know, and we can
find the sequence to add to the

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:28.210
reference set.

00:45:28.210 --> 00:45:32.710
So, what this will mean that in
the future, if you submit

00:45:32.710 --> 00:45:36.430
another sequence that has that
mutation, it will not throw up a

00:45:36.430 --> 00:45:39.510
VADR alert, and it will go right
through the process without

00:45:39.510 --> 00:45:41.210
anybody having to review it.

00:45:41.210 --> 00:45:44.790
So, we'd really like this
community's help in analyzing

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:47.930
these, if at all possible.

00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:51.990
If you do have one of these
sequences where you have

00:45:51.990 --> 00:45:56.280
confirmed that what is flagged
as an alert is correct, please

00:45:56.280 --> 00:45:57.300
let us know.

00:45:57.300 --> 00:46:01.940
You can write to GB-Admin, and
you can just let us know the

00:46:01.940 --> 00:46:05.260
read coverage over the area of
the mutation.

00:46:05.260 --> 00:46:08.240
And if you've not already
provided the SRR number, it

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:11.980
would be great to have that
because one of the themes that

00:46:11.980 --> 00:46:15.110
we've had in several of these
talks is trying to link the

00:46:15.110 --> 00:46:19.490
assembled data back to the read
data and assess whether there's

00:46:19.490 --> 00:46:20.490
any anomalies.

00:46:20.490 --> 00:46:23.860
So we really are trying to
promote getting that SRR

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:25.110
information.

00:46:25.110 --> 00:46:28.610
And that will actually lead into
Yuriy talking.

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:30.300
&gt;&gt; Okay, great.

00:46:30.300 --> 00:46:36.300
So, as I was saying, what we
want to do is create connections

00:46:36.300 --> 00:46:41.830
between GenBank and SRA to make
it a lot easier for users to

00:46:41.830 --> 00:46:46.870
either get the GenBank data sets
or get the SRA data sets.

00:46:46.870 --> 00:46:53.390
It's very common for me to hear
a request from users to go back

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:57.320
and forth, either to get the raw
data, so that they can confirm

00:46:57.320 --> 00:47:03.020
findings that they saw in the
GenBank record, or be able to

00:47:03.020 --> 00:47:07.710
first look at the GenBank record
before having to download a

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:13.180
large SRA data set and have to
spend lots of time assembling it

00:47:13.180 --> 00:47:17.720
and doing other things and that
would instead prefer to look at

00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:19.910
GenBank submissions.

00:47:19.910 --> 00:47:27.570
So, if you are submitting to
NCBI and you are submitting

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:32.640
COVID data sets, we did create
specifically an umbrella

00:47:32.640 --> 00:47:35.540
BioProject for all SPHERES
submissions, which you can see

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:37.190
on the slide.

00:47:37.190 --> 00:47:42.660
And if you are starting out a
new project yourself, you can

00:47:42.660 --> 00:47:45.950
enter this umbrella BioProject
as part of your initial

00:47:45.950 --> 00:47:48.200
submission to register your own
BioProject.

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:52.800
This way, all of the BioProjects
submitted under SPHERES would be

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:57.950
grouped together, making it that
much more easier to retrieve the

00:47:57.950 --> 00:48:01.820
entire data set, if somebody
wanted to process things in

00:48:01.820 --> 00:48:02.820
bulk.

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:09.070
So, you know, you can make sure
to use the same BioProjects and

00:48:09.070 --> 00:48:12.470
BioSamples in both your SRA and
GenBank submissions to make it

00:48:12.470 --> 00:48:18.030
easier for us to interconnect
the data sets on our back ends.

00:48:18.030 --> 00:48:20.030
Next slide, please.

00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:25.580
So, if you're getting started
with SRA submissions and you've

00:48:25.580 --> 00:48:31.869
never submitted to us before, we
are the raw data repository, and

00:48:31.869 --> 00:48:36.050
as you're submitting to SRA, if
you've never submitted a data

00:48:36.050 --> 00:48:39.820
project, you can submit your
BioProject and BioSample, which

00:48:39.820 --> 00:48:44.490
is basically your study and
sample registration as part of

00:48:44.490 --> 00:48:46.330
the SRA submission.

00:48:46.330 --> 00:48:49.570
So you don't need to do this
piecemeal if you have all of

00:48:49.570 --> 00:48:53.090
your data together at the start
of the submission.

00:48:53.090 --> 00:48:56.410
We do have very flexible upload
options.

00:48:56.410 --> 00:49:00.680
We have web uploads through
basic drag-and-drop.

00:49:00.680 --> 00:49:03.400
We have an FTP upload method.

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:08.230
And we can get data from AWS
buckets directly.

00:49:08.230 --> 00:49:12.501
We are working on getting data
from GCP buckets, but that's

00:49:12.501 --> 00:49:15.900
going to come probably next
month, as we still have some

00:49:15.900 --> 00:49:18.320
development work to finish up
for that.

00:49:18.320 --> 00:49:21.750
But there are multiple ways to
upload to us.

00:49:21.750 --> 00:49:26.710
And of course, we have the same
programmatic submission pathway

00:49:26.710 --> 00:49:28.260
as GenBank.

00:49:28.260 --> 00:49:33.011
So we use the same back-end
system to do all of the

00:49:33.011 --> 00:49:34.011
submissions programmatically.

00:49:34.011 --> 00:49:39.530
So, should you want to submit
10,000 or more samples at the

00:49:39.530 --> 00:49:44.510
same time or over the next year
or two, that is definitely a

00:49:44.510 --> 00:49:47.400
great way to go about it so you
can drive your submissions off

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:52.610
of your own limbs or database
that you use for storing and

00:49:52.610 --> 00:49:56.580
organizing your own internal
data sets.

00:49:56.580 --> 00:49:59.930
Next slide, please.

00:49:59.930 --> 00:50:04.200
As you create SRA submissions,
once it's submitted and SRA has

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:09.660
had a chance to process it, we
do provide a way to modify the

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:13.440
contents of the submission
through a web interface.

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:18.730
If you go to our submission
system, it's called Manage Data,

00:50:18.730 --> 00:50:21.070
and you have several things you
can do.

00:50:21.070 --> 00:50:24.760
You can create reviewer links if
you're doing submissions that

00:50:24.760 --> 00:50:25.950
are part of a publication.

00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:30.540
So you can send a link to a
reviewer so they can take a look

00:50:30.540 --> 00:50:35.670
at the samples and the
descriptions of the data sets

00:50:35.670 --> 00:50:41.070
you've submitted to us and how
it applies to your publication.

00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:43.570
And you can edit your title,
descriptions of your

00:50:43.570 --> 00:50:44.570
BioProjects.

00:50:44.570 --> 00:50:49.030
You can add grants and
publication links after the fact

00:50:49.030 --> 00:50:50.030
of submitting the publication.

00:50:50.030 --> 00:50:55.610
And of course, you can modify
SRA data, as well.

00:50:55.610 --> 00:50:58.790
We don't allow modification of
the actual files you submitted

00:50:58.790 --> 00:51:01.120
that requires a new submission.

00:51:01.120 --> 00:51:05.490
But any type of metadata that's
text-based, you can modify it

00:51:05.490 --> 00:51:11.140
through the web portal, and it
usually gets processed and

00:51:11.140 --> 00:51:14.640
incorporated very quickly,
usually within minutes.

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:18.270
It might take a little bit
longer for it to appear on our

00:51:18.270 --> 00:51:23.260
web pages, but that just is a
matter of getting indexed.

00:51:23.260 --> 00:51:27.460
And I guess I wanted to throw up
some statistics.

00:51:27.460 --> 00:51:32.830
So, we've had roughly 11,000
runs from USA.

00:51:32.830 --> 00:51:38.780
Overall, we have around 100,000
runs from around the world.

00:51:38.780 --> 00:51:41.800
And the 100,000 does include
USA.

00:51:41.800 --> 00:51:47.020
So, we do have a large amount of
COVID data that's available for

00:51:47.020 --> 00:51:48.020
you.

00:51:48.020 --> 00:51:51.410
And the best part about it is
all of this data is part of the

00:51:51.410 --> 00:51:53.920
AWS open data program.

00:51:53.920 --> 00:51:57.570
So it's available to download
for free.

00:51:57.570 --> 00:52:00.530
You don't have to be in the
Cloud to use it.

00:52:00.530 --> 00:52:05.010
You can download it to your
local compute form and process

00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:06.460
the data there.

00:52:06.460 --> 00:52:09.770
And we do provide multiple
different formats there.

00:52:09.770 --> 00:52:14.540
So feel free to check it out and
let us know when you need

00:52:14.540 --> 00:52:15.540
feedback.

00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:17.870
Next slide, please.

00:52:17.870 --> 00:52:21.840
So, the last slide is We're Here
For You.

00:52:21.840 --> 00:52:27.430
We've done lots of things with
your input that have made very

00:52:27.430 --> 00:52:30.740
good improvements to our system,
such as the programmatic

00:52:30.740 --> 00:52:33.920
submission options, the file
upload drag-and-drop for the

00:52:33.920 --> 00:52:34.920
system.

00:52:34.920 --> 00:52:38.190
We've had multiple documentation
edits thanks to the feedback

00:52:38.190 --> 00:52:40.770
we've received from the SPHERES
group.

00:52:40.770 --> 00:52:44.200
But we do still need feedback
from you guys to continue

00:52:44.200 --> 00:52:45.900
improving our services.

00:52:45.900 --> 00:52:51.190
We certainly have some BioSample
linkages and data flow

00:52:51.190 --> 00:52:54.030
discussions for GenBank
submissions.

00:52:54.030 --> 00:52:58.290
We've briefly talked about how
to do programmatic submissions

00:52:58.290 --> 00:53:01.400
for SRA and GenBank at the same
time.

00:53:01.400 --> 00:53:05.710
And for SRA, we do have what,
for now, we're dubbing the

00:53:05.710 --> 00:53:10.860
"human scrubber" tool for being
able to identify human reads in

00:53:10.860 --> 00:53:15.270
raw data files and being able to
remove them so that if you're

00:53:15.270 --> 00:53:18.070
submitting data to SRA and
you're worried about human data

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:21.750
being present there, this tool
could help you remove those

00:53:21.750 --> 00:53:23.010
reads.

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:25.870
But we definitely need feedback
on that tool.

00:53:25.870 --> 00:53:30.610
So if you're interested for any
of these things, please just

00:53:30.610 --> 00:53:34.590
contact us and tell us you want
to be a tester or provide

00:53:34.590 --> 00:53:37.780
feedback, and we'll definitely
schedule an interview with you

00:53:37.780 --> 00:53:40.650
so that we can hear all of your
feedback.

00:53:40.650 --> 00:53:41.780
So, thank you.

00:53:41.780 --> 00:53:47.340
&gt;&gt; I don't know if we'll have
time for a Q&amp;A.

00:53:47.340 --> 00:53:50.130
I realize we're at time exactly
now.

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:55.050
But the good news is I think we
will continue to have NCBI

00:53:55.050 --> 00:53:56.430
updates and discussions.

00:53:56.430 --> 00:54:00.830
For one thing, I think we can
continue some of the Q&amp;A online.

00:54:00.830 --> 00:54:05.050
But I think NCBI's committed to
doing a series of updates over

00:54:05.050 --> 00:54:07.860
the next couple months on
different topics.

00:54:07.860 --> 00:54:10.840
So I think it'll be a great
opportunity to pull back the

00:54:10.840 --> 00:54:13.410
curtain on some of these and
really provide some user

00:54:13.410 --> 00:54:16.860
feedback, which I think is
absolutely critical on the NCBI

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:17.860
side.

00:54:17.860 --> 00:54:22.640
I'd really like to thank Lydia,
Eric, Linda, Yuriy, Anita,

00:54:22.640 --> 00:54:23.640
Eileen.

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:26.080
Am I missing anybody?

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:28.430
Great work from the NCBI team.

00:54:28.430 --> 00:54:30.710
I think this has been very
insightful for me.

00:54:30.710 --> 00:54:34.090
I think just given the quality
and number of questions, I think

00:54:34.090 --> 00:54:37.380
there's a lot of interest in how
to work better with the NCBI

00:54:37.380 --> 00:54:40.980
resources and to ensure that our
sequences are submitted and

00:54:40.980 --> 00:54:41.980
annotated properly.

00:54:41.980 --> 00:54:44.170
So we're excited by that.

00:54:44.170 --> 00:54:47.740
Thank you all, and we will talk
soon.

00:54:47.740 --> 00:54:51.860
Lydia or others, do you have any
sign-offs or anything you need

00:54:51.860 --> 00:54:52.860
to add?

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:54.359
&gt;&gt; Thank you from NCBI.

00:54:54.359 --> 00:54:57.450
&gt;&gt; Alright, thank you all, and
we'll see you all next week.

